On 2008-03-17, dogscoff <dogscoff@eudoramail.com> wrote:
> On Mar 14, 6:09 pm, money cat <mnard...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> In my opinion, polypiling as it stands right now is too powerful.
> It's worth noting that in slashem, polymorphed items (and monster)
> eventually revert back to their original form. I know some people here
> disregard any and all slashem features out of hand but I think this is
> a good one. The thought of having your silver daggers turn back into a
> pile of darts in the middle gehemonoemenon is quite deliciously evil.

And just means that people end up not bothering with polymorph at all
for these purposes.  But making a bunch of silver daggers isn't the
'abusive' facet of polymorph people were discussing.

> Obviously one counter to poly-reversion is to just use the polypiled
> item immediately (scrolls/ potions/ markers/ eating rings) before it
> gets a chance to revert. This is fine as far as I'm concerned, because
> it's certainly no more powerful than vanilla polymorph.

This is the abusive part though.  Scrolls, spellbooks, eating the rings,
using the markers -- these are the bits that are really useful.  Slash'EM's
"solution" does nothing to fix this, because they missed the point of
polypiling in the first place.

(In fact, the 'reversion' of spellbooks would be even more beneficial to
the Wizard, who can poly his spellbooks to there and back again
without actually losing his starting spells!)

> However, even with fixing as it is, polypiling in slashem is still
> less productive and more hassle than in vanilla.

As stated elsewhere, I will be looking at adding risks, not removing
rewards in this instance.  Slash'EM took the route of "remove rewards",
with the end result of not really having much effect other than making
it harder to get specialized rare weaponry like silver daggers and/or
specialized rare rings that you intended to wear, like conflict.

